WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.229
hello-devops answers requests all day, and a few of them always fail.

2
00:00:07.433 --> 00:00:10.503
So at 3 a.m., how many failures are too many?

3
00:00:10.503 --> 00:00:12.481
When should a person wake up?

4
00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:16.627
First, decide what good means, seen from the user's side.

5
00:00:16.833 --> 00:00:20.888
A good event is a response to / that is not 5xx.

6
00:00:20.888 --> 00:00:23.844
A valid event is any response to /.

7
00:00:24.067 --> 00:00:29.343
A 404 for a mistyped URL is the user's mistake, so it does not count.

8
00:00:29.567 --> 00:00:35.547
Good events over valid events: that ratio is the SLI, the service level indicator.

9
00:00:36.967 --> 00:00:47.000
An SLO is a target for that ratio over a time window: 99.9% of requests to / succeed, over the last 30 days.

10
00:00:47.233 --> 00:00:57.459
The rest is the error budget, the failure the SLO still allows: 100% minus 99.9%, so 0.1%.

11
00:00:58.267 --> 00:01:01.815
Say hello-devops gets 200,000 requests a day.

12
00:01:02.033 --> 00:01:06.691
Thirty days hold 6 million requests, and 6,000 of them may fail.

13
00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:11.621
If everything fails at once, that is 43.2 minutes of full outage.

14
00:01:11.833 --> 00:01:15.097
Now one bad deploy breaks everything for 20 minutes.

15
00:01:15.097 --> 00:01:17.607
That alone spends almost half the month.

16
00:01:18.433 --> 00:01:22.106
The budget gives developers and operators the same number.

17
00:01:22.106 --> 00:01:25.081
While budget is left, you ship at normal speed.

18
00:01:25.300 --> 00:01:29.787
When it is spent, you pause risky releases until reliability is back.

19
00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:35.188
But at 3 a.m., the question is how fast the budget is going.

20
00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:39.908
That is the burn rate: the current error ratio, divided by the budget.

21
00:01:40.133 --> 00:01:43.276
Draw the budget over the 30 days, full at the top.

22
00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:47.557
If 0.1% of requests fail, that is burn rate 1.

23
00:01:47.557 --> 00:01:50.468
The budget lasts exactly 30 days.

24
00:01:50.733 --> 00:01:53.444
At 0.6%, the burn rate is 6.

25
00:01:53.444 --> 00:01:56.251
The budget is gone in 5 days.

26
00:01:56.467 --> 00:01:59.520
At 1.44%, the burn rate is 14.4.

27
00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:02.859
The budget is gone in about 2 days.

28
00:02:05.267 --> 00:02:06.860
Why the odd 14.4?

29
00:02:06.860 --> 00:02:08.266
Work backwards.

30
00:02:08.266 --> 00:02:14.545
At burn rate 1, the budget is spread over all 720 hours of 30 days.

31
00:02:14.767 --> 00:02:19.760
Page once a single hour has spent 2% of the budget.

32
00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:23.676
2% of 720 hours is 14.4 hours of budget.

33
00:02:23.900 --> 00:02:27.875
Spend that in one hour, and that is burn rate 14.4.

34
00:02:28.133 --> 00:02:31.624
These numbers come from Google's SRE Workbook.

35
00:02:31.624 --> 00:02:35.266
Page at 14.4 over one hour, or 6 over six hours.

36
00:02:35.266 --> 00:02:37.391
Ticket at 1 over three days.

37
00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:40.788
Sort every alert into three groups.

38
00:02:40.788 --> 00:02:44.130
Page: users hurt now, and a human must act now.

39
00:02:44.130 --> 00:02:46.547
Ticket: a human, in working hours.

40
00:02:46.547 --> 00:02:48.823
Everything else: dashboard only.

41
00:02:49.033 --> 00:02:53.276
A bad hour: 2% of requests fail for 60 minutes.

42
00:02:53.276 --> 00:02:55.532
Burn rate 20, above 14.4.

43
00:02:55.733 --> 00:02:57.041
So it pages.

44
00:02:57.267 --> 00:02:59.738
A slow leak: 0.3% fail all week.

45
00:02:59.738 --> 00:03:02.672
Burn rate 3: the budget lasts 10 days.

46
00:03:02.672 --> 00:03:07.228
Nobody needs to wake up, but someone must fix it this week.

47
00:03:07.433 --> 00:03:15.205
A server at 90% CPU that answers every request in 50 ms is simply doing its job.

48
00:03:16.333 --> 00:03:21.767
CPU, memory, single restarts: these are causes, and many never hurt anyone.

49
00:03:21.767 --> 00:03:23.579
They go on the dashboard.

50
00:03:23.800 --> 00:03:29.481
Page on causes, and people learn that pages are noise, and ignore the real one too.

51
00:03:29.700 --> 00:03:35.893
An SLO turns raw requests into a budget, and the burn rate decides who gets woken up.
